# Express — the Node arm. See scripts/demo-targets/sympy.cmds for the two
# rules every command here has to satisfy.
#
# All three leave the tree byte-identical. Express gitignores node_modules,
# coverage/ and .nyc_output, so the ordinary test paths write nothing git can
# see, and the env fingerprint is unmoved because none of them touch
# node_modules. Measured before and after: tree 4a68f512..., env-fp f7c9b32d...
# for all three.
#
# Two commands, not three, and both of the obvious candidates for a third were
# measured and rejected:
#
# `npm run lint` straddles the floor. Standalone it took 754 ms; inside the
# probe, with the filesystem warm, 495 ms. A command that lands on either side
# of 500 ms depending on the weather is the exact failure the floor check
# exists to catch — it would be served on the rehearsal and silently
# unservable on stage.
#
# `npm run test-cov` and `npm run test-ci` are slow enough, but nyc writes
# coverage/ and .nyc_output/, both gitignored, so the tree hash cannot see
# them and neither can the Node fingerprint, which only reads the lockfile and
# node_modules. They would be SERVED, and they should not be: the transcript
# replays and the coverage report does not. That gap is real and belongs in
# ISSUES_FOR_TOM.md, not in a demo that claims the gate catches everything.

# KNOWN, MEASURED: shadow verification intermittently reports a divergence on
# this target, and after investigation it is REAL in the bytes and harmless in
# meaning. mocha's spec reporter annotates a test with its duration only when
# the test exceeds the "slow" threshold, so which tests carry an annotation
# depends on machine load:
#
#   -   ✔ should not stack overflow with many registered routes
#   +   ✔ should not stack overflow with many registered routes (76ms)
#
# One run printed a duration and the other printed none. Normalization erases
# durations; it cannot invent one that was never printed. So this is not the
# known cross-worktree path defect (this output contains no absolute paths at
# all — checked) and not the whole-millisecond gap in norm.go (fixed, and five
# concurrent worktrees now agree 4/4 after normalization).
#
# It is worth having rather than hiding. The purity gate measures STATE, and
# this command is genuinely pure by state — the tree and the fingerprint do not
# move. What moves is the transcript, and no amount of state hashing can see
# that. Catching it is exactly what shadow verification is for.

#expect:served
npm test
#expect:served
npm run test-tap
